Introduction

The transition to a multiplanetary civilization demands cognitive infrastructure fundamentally different from the engagement-maximizing social networks of the present era. Knowledge must flow across interplanetary distances with minimal latency, scientific collaboration must transcend organizational boundaries, and the collective intelligence of humanity must be orchestrated to solve problems of unprecedented complexity. This manuscript develops a rigorous mathematical foundation for such a network, treating it simultaneously as a dynamic Riemannian manifold, an optimal transport network, a higher-order collaboration structure, a controlled linear system, a mean-field game, a stochastic block model, a signal processing domain, and a representation-learning environment.

The central thesis is that the network can be designed to minimize the distance—measured in Wasserstein metrics, spectral gaps, and entropy-weighted divergences—between problems and their potential solutions. Eight interconnected proposals form a unified theory, each anchored in deep theorems with explicit proof architectures. The mathematical tools span discrete Ricci flow, Kantorovich duality, hypergraph spectral theory, nonbacktracking matrices, mean-field game master equations, submodular optimization, and graph signal processing. Cross-cutting principles of entropy, spectral expansion, and curvature unify the framework.

