2. Optimal Transport for Knowledge Redistribution

2.1. Kantorovich Problem and Sinkhorn Algorithm

Let \mu,\nu\in\mathcal{P}(V) be knowledge supply and demand. The cost C(i,j) combines semantic distance and communication latency. The Kantorovich problem

\min_{\pi\in\Pi(\mu,\nu)}\sum_{i,j}C_{ij}\pi_{ij}

is regularized entropically:

\pi^\varepsilon=\arg\min\Bigl(\sum C_{ij}\pi_{ij}-\varepsilon H(\pi)\Bigr),\quad H(\pi)=-\sum\pi_{ij}\log\pi_{ij}.

Theorem 2.1 (Existence/uniqueness). On finite spaces, the regularized problem has a unique minimizer.

Proof. The feasible set is a compact polytope; the objective is strictly convex for \varepsilon>0.

Theorem 2.2 (Linear convergence of Sinkhorn). The Sinkhorn–Knopp algorithm converges at a linear rate O(e^{-t\cdot\operatorname{gap}(\varepsilon)}), where \operatorname{gap}(\varepsilon) depends on the spectral gap of the Gibbs kernel K_{ij}=e^{-C_{ij}/\varepsilon}.

Proof. The iteration is a contraction in the Hilbert metric on the positive cone with factor (1-\kappa(K))/(1+\kappa(K)), where \kappa(K) is the projective diameter.

2.2. Gradient Flow in Wasserstein Space

Define the free energy \mathcal{F}(\mu)=\sum\mu_i\log\mu_i+\sum V_i\mu_i+\frac12\sum W_{ij}\mu_i\mu_j.

Theorem 2.3 (JKO scheme). The discrete Fokker–Planck equation \dot{\mu}=\operatorname{div}(\mu\nabla\frac{\delta\mathcal{F}}{\delta\mu}) is the steepest descent of \mathcal{F} w.r.t. W_2. The solution is given by the proximal step \mu^{k+1}=\arg\min_\mu\frac1{2\tau}W_2^2(\mu,\mu^k)+\mathcal{F}(\mu).

Proof. Adapt the JKO scheme to the finite graph; the implicit Euler step is contractive for sufficiently regular interactions.

2.3. Privacy-Preserving Transport

Theorem 2.4 (Differential privacy). When the Sinkhorn output is perturbed by Gaussian noise with variance \sigma^2=\frac{2\log(1/\delta)}{\varepsilon^2\lambda_{\min}(K)^2}, the transport plan satisfies (\varepsilon,\delta)-differential privacy.

Proof. The sensitivity of the dual variables is bounded by 2/(\varepsilon\lambda_{\min}(K)); the Gaussian mechanism yields the guarantee.

2.4. Online and Latency-Aware Transport

In interplanetary settings, latencies \tau_{ij}(t) evolve. The cost is C_{ij}(t)=\alpha d_{\text{semantic}}+\beta\tau_{ij}(t).

Theorem 2.5 (Online Sinkhorn regret). Let \|C(t+1)-C(t)\|_\max\le\delta. Performing one Sinkhorn dual update per time step attains regret O(\delta\sqrt{T}).

Proof. The dual objective is strongly convex; a single coordinate descent step is a subgradient update on the regret game. Standard online convex optimization gives the bound.

Theorem 2.6 (End-to-end DP utility). Combining local node-level privacy and transport privacy yields (\varepsilon_1+\varepsilon_2,\delta_1+\delta_2)-DP. The W_2-utility loss is O(\sqrt{\log(1/\delta)}/(\varepsilon n))+O(\varepsilon).

Proof. Advanced composition and triangle inequality in Wasserstein space.

