3. Hypergraph Models for Higher-Order Collaboration

3.1. Edge-Dependent Vertex Weights (EDVW) and Laplacian

A hypergraph H=(V,\mathcal{E}) with weights \gamma_e(v) for each vertex in hyperedge e gives the Laplacian

L_H = D_v - A W D_e^{-1} A^T,

with D_v=\operatorname{diag}(\sum_{e\ni v}\omega(e)\gamma_e(v)), D_e=\operatorname{diag}(\sum_{v\in e}\gamma_e(v)). The normalized Laplacian is \mathcal{L}_{\text{sym}}=\Pi^{-1/2}L_H\Pi^{-1/2}.

3.2. Hypergraph Cheeger Inequality

Define the hypergraph conductance

\Phi(S)=\frac{\sum_{e\in\partial S}\omega(e)\min\{\sum_{v\in S\cap e}\gamma_e(v),\sum_{v\in\bar{S}\cap e}\gamma_e(v)\}}{\min\{\operatorname{vol}(S),\operatorname{vol}(\bar{S})\}}.

Theorem 3.1 (Hypergraph Cheeger). For the normalized EDVW Laplacian,

\frac{\Phi(H)^2}{2}\le\lambda_2\le 2\Phi(H).

Proof. The lower bound uses a test vector x_v=\sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(\bar{S})/\operatorname{vol}(S)} for v\in S and the negative root otherwise. Substituting into the Rayleigh quotient yields \lambda_2\le 2\Phi(S). For the upper bound, sweep over thresholds of the Fiedler vector; the co-area formula for hypergraphs yields a set with conductance at most \sqrt{2\lambda_2}.

Theorem 3.2 (Robustness). If \lambda_2\ge\gamma, after removing any \beta n vertices, the giant component size is at least n(1-\beta/\gamma).

Proof. By the Cheeger inequality, the isoperimetric constant is \ge\gamma/2. A hypergraph expander mixing lemma bounds the volume of disconnected pieces.

3.3. Non-Uniform Hypergraphs and the Nonbacktracking Operator

Real collaboration hypergraphs have varying edge sizes. Define the nonbacktracking operator B on directed edge-vertex pairs (e\to v) by

B_{(e\to v),(e'\to v')}= \mathbf{1}_{v\in e'}\mathbf{1}_{v'\in e}\mathbf{1}_{v\neq v'}\omega(e').

Theorem 3.3 (Ihara–Bass for hypergraphs). Let A,D be the clique-expanded adjacency and degree matrices. Then

\det(I-zB)=\det(I-zA+z^2(D-I))\prod_{e\in\mathcal{E}}(1-z^{|e|})^{|e|-2}.

Proof. By induction on hyperedge size, factoring the determinant along cycles; the product accounts for the internal structure of each hyperedge.

Theorem 3.4 (Spectral threshold for hypergraph SBM). For a hypergraph SBM with size distribution p_s and intra/inter probabilities a_s,b_s, nonbacktracking spectral clustering succeeds non-trivially iff

\sum_s p_s\frac{(a_s-b_s)^2}{2(a_s+b_s)}>1.

Proof. The bulk eigenvalue of B is \sqrt{c} with c=\sum p_s(a_s+b_s)/2. Informative eigenvalues are (a_s-b_s)/2; escape requires their magnitude to exceed the bulk radius.

Theorem 3.5 (Robustness to missing hyperedges). If each hyperedge is observed with probability q, the threshold becomes q\sum_s p_s\frac{(a_s-b_s)^2}{2(a_s+b_s)}>1, and misclassification error is O(1/(q\lambda_2)).

Proof. Erasure scales connection probabilities; Davis–Kahan perturbation theory bounds the eigenvector perturbation.

