4. Spectral Controllability of Knowledge Networks

4.1. Linear Control on Graphs

The knowledge state x\in\mathbb{R}^n evolves as \dot{x}=-Lx+Bu, with Laplacian L and control matrix B\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}.

Theorem 4.1 (Fiedler eigenvalue control). To minimize consensus time \tau, one must maximize \lambda_2(L+BKB^T) via feedback K; then \tau\propto 1/\lambda_2^{\text{target}}.

Theorem 4.2 (Minimum energy control). The energy x_f^TW_c^{-1}x_f with Gramian W_c=\int_0^Te^{-Lt}BB^Te^{-L^Tt}dt is minimized when control nodes correspond to the largest entries of the Fiedler vector.

Proof. Expand W_c in the eigenbasis; each control node contributes b_i^Tv_kv_k^Tb_i/\lambda_k to the trace of W_c^{-1}. The Fiedler vector weights the smallest positive eigenvalue most heavily.

Theorem 4.3 (Controllability–observability duality). (L,B) is controllable iff (L,B^T) is observable; W_c solves the Lyapunov equation -LW_c-W_cL^T+BB^T=0.

4.2. Dynamic Edge Weights as Control

Reparametrizing w_{ij}=\phi(\theta_{ij}) with learnable \theta yields a nonlinear system linearizable around the consensus manifold. A dual-basis representation gives closed-form optimal \theta to achieve a target knowledge distribution.

