5. Mean-Field Games for Collective Innovation

5.1. Mean-Field Equilibrium (MFE)

A population of N agents has state x_i\in\mathbb{R}^d:

dx_i = f(x_i,\alpha_i,m)dt+\sigma dW_i,

where m is the mean field. Each agent minimizes J_i(\alpha;m)=\mathbb{E}[\int_0^T L(x,\alpha,m)ds+G(x(T),m(T))]. An MFE (\hat{\alpha},\bar{m}) satisfies optimality and consistency.

Theorem 5.1 (Existence via Schauder). Under Lipschitz conditions and convex Hamiltonian, the HJB–FP system has a solution.

Proof. Define the operator T: freeze m, solve HJB backward, FP forward. T maps a compact convex set of measure-valued curves into itself and is continuous; Schauder’s theorem gives a fixed point.

Theorem 5.2 (\varepsilon-Nash approximation). If N agents follow the MFE policy, the game is an \varepsilon-Nash equilibrium with \varepsilon=O(1/\sqrt{N}).

Proof. Propagation of chaos gives W_2(m^N,\bar{m})\le C/\sqrt{N}; the deviation cost is bounded by this distance owing to Lipschitz continuity.

5.2. Entropy-Regularized MFG and Learning

Theorem 5.3 (Uniqueness). Adding an entropic cost on actions makes the HJB strictly convex and the mapping T a strict contraction in total variation, ensuring a unique equilibrium.

Theorem 5.4 (Policy gradient convergence). Variance-reduced policy gradient finds an \varepsilon-Nash equilibrium in O(1/\varepsilon^2) iterations.

Proof. The gradient is Lipschitz; SVRG achieves O(1/K) convergence to stationarity, translating to the Nash bound.

5.3. Common Noise and the Master Equation

Interplanetary networks experience shared stochastic events (solar flares). The dynamics become

dX_t^i = f(X_t^i,\alpha_t^i,m_t)dt+\sigma dW_t^i+\varepsilon dW_t^0,

with common noise W_t^0. The mean field is now the conditional law m_t=\mathcal{L}(X_t^i|\mathcal{F}_t^0). The value function solves a stochastic PDE (master equation).

Theorem 5.5 (Common-noise MFE). Under the previous assumptions and \varepsilon>0, there exists a unique classical solution to the master equation, and the associated MFE is unique.

Proof sketch. Conditional FBSDE yields a decoupling field U(t,x,m); the mapping on conditional laws is contractive in the metric \mathbb{E}[\sup_t W_2(m_t,m_t')^2]^{1/2}.

Theorem 5.6 (Learning with common noise). Policy gradient with conditional mean-field approximation converges to \varepsilon-Nash in O(1/\varepsilon^2) iterations, with an additional bias O(\varepsilon^2).

