6. Stochastic Block Models for Community Detection

6.1. Exact Recovery Threshold

For the symmetric two-community SBM with intra-community probability a/n and inter-community b/n, exact recovery is possible iff \sqrt{a}-\sqrt{b}>\sqrt{2}.

Theorem 6.1 (Threshold). The condition is both necessary and sufficient.

Proof. Necessity: genie-aided total variation bound fails below the threshold. Sufficiency: the nonbacktracking matrix B has an informative eigenvalue \mu_2=\frac{a-b}{2} that escapes the bulk radius \sqrt{(a+b)/2} exactly when the condition holds; spectral clustering on B achieves exact recovery.

Theorem 6.2 (SDP exact recovery). The SDP \max_{Z\succeq0, Z_{ii}=1}\langle A,Z\rangle also recovers the communities exactly above the threshold.

Proof. Construct a dual certificate matrix Y with \lambda_{\min}(Y-A+\Lambda)\ge0 using concentration inequalities.

Theorem 6.3 (Adversarial robustness). After O(\log n) edge perturbations, the nonbacktracking matrix still yields exact recovery under the same asymptotic condition.

Proof. The eigenvalue shift is O(\sqrt{\log n}) while the gap is \Omega(\sqrt{n}), leaving the informative eigenvalue outside the bulk.

