7. Graph Signal Processing and Influence Maximization

7.1. Graph Fourier Transform and Filters

A graph signal x\in\mathbb{R}^n is transformed via the eigenvectors of \mathcal{L}_{\text{sym}}: \hat{x}=U^Tx. A graph filter is H(S)x=\sum_{k=0}^K h_k S^k x with shift S=I-\mathcal{L}_{\text{sym}}. The heat equation is x(t)=e^{-tL}x(0).

Theorem 7.1 (Uncertainty principle). Let \|x\|_0 be spatial and \|\hat{x}\|_0 spectral support. Then \|x\|_0\|\hat{x}\|_0\ge 1/\mu_U^2, where \mu_U=\max_{i,k}|u_k(i)|.

Proof. Frame coherence bound for two orthonormal bases.

Theorem 7.2 (Filter stability). For a K-th order polynomial filter and Laplacian perturbation \|\Delta L\|\le\delta, \|H(L+\Delta L)x-H(L)x\|\le K C_H\delta\|x\|.

Proof. Binomial expansion of (L+\Delta L)^k.

Theorem 7.3 (Sampling and exact reconstruction). A bandlimited signal (supported on first r eigenvectors) can be exactly reconstructed from a sampling set S that is a uniqueness set for the subspace, with stability governed by the frame bound \kappa=B/A.

7.2. Influence Maximization with Latencies

In the Independent Cascade model with edge delays \tau_{uv}, the latency-aware spread \sigma_L(S) is the expected number of activations within time T_{\max}.

Theorem 7.4 (Submodularity and greedy). \sigma_L(S) is monotone and submodular; the greedy algorithm yields \sigma_L(S_{\text{greedy}})\ge(1-1/e)\sigma_L(S_{\text{opt}})-O(\epsilon).

Proof. Live-edge graph representation preserves submodularity; Monte Carlo estimation adds \epsilon error.

Theorem 7.5 (Reverse Reachable Sets). To achieve a (1-1/e-\epsilon)-approximation with probability 1-\delta, R=O(\frac{(1-1/e)n\log n}{\epsilon^2\sigma_L(S_{\text{opt}})}) RR-sets suffice, each generable in expected path length time by reverse Dijkstra limited to T_{\max}.

