8.1. Entropy-Gated Attention

Node representations z_v from a GNN yield class probabilities p(c|v) and entropy H(v)=-\sum_c p(c|v)\log p(c|v). Attention weights are

\alpha_{ij}=\operatorname{softmax}\Bigl(\frac{QK^T}{\sqrt{d_k}}\Bigr)_{ij}\cdot\Bigl(1-\frac{H(v_j)}{H_{\max}}\Bigr),

down-weighting high-entropy (uncertain) neighbors.

Theorem 8.1 (Variance reduction). The variance of predicted probabilities across the neighborhood is reduced by a factor (1-\bar{H}_i/H_{\max})^2 compared to standard attention.

Proof. Importance-sampling interpretation: effective sample size increases when noisy nodes are down-weighted.

Theorem 8.2 (InfoNCE lower bound). For a hypergraph contrastive loss, \mathcal{L}\ge \log|\mathcal{N}|-\frac1\tau\mathbb{E}_{\text{pos}}[\operatorname{sim}(z_i,z_j)]+\text{const}, and the positive term is bounded by the probability of staying within a hyperedge, related to the spectral gap of the hypergraph Laplacian.

Theorem 8.3 (Lipschitz continuity). The dual-dependency encoder f is L_f-Lipschitz in structural perturbations \|\Delta A\|\le\delta, with L_f depending on GNN depth and weight norms.

Proof. Compose contraction factors of each GNN layer; spectral perturbation bound on the normalized adjacency.

Theorem 8.4 (Gradient stability). The entropy gate does not introduce vanishing/exploding gradients; the norm is O(d_k^{-1/2}).

Proof. Backpropagation through softmax and the entropy derivative, which is bounded by \|W\|\log C.

