Theorem 9.1 (Coupled stability). Assume: (i) micro-dynamics satisfy MFE conditions; (ii) graph rewiring preserves \lambda_2\ge\gamma>0; (iii) the GNN learning has Lipschitz constant L_\theta<\gamma/2. Then the coupled system converges to a neighborhood of size O(L_\theta/\gamma) around the equilibrium (m^*,G^*,\theta^*).

Proof. Build a Lyapunov function \mathcal{V}=W_2(m,m^*)^2+\|G-G^*\|_F^2+\|\theta-\theta^*\|^2. Micro-dynamics contract W_2 at rate \lambda_2; rewiring contracts G at rate \gamma; learning is L_\theta-Lipschitz. By the small-gain theorem, the cascade is stable if L_\theta<\gamma/2.

Theorem 9.2 (End-to-end privacy-utility). The integrated system satisfies (\varepsilon_{\text{total}},\delta_{\text{total}})-DP with \varepsilon_{\text{total}}=\sum_p\varepsilon_p. The knowledge dissemination rate is lower bounded by

\lambda_{\text{eff}} = \lambda_2(G^*) - O\Bigl(\frac{\sqrt{\log(1/\delta_{\text{total}})}}{\varepsilon_{\text{total}} n}\Bigr).

Proof. Advanced composition of privacy mechanisms; each mechanism perturbs the Laplacian, and Weyl’s inequality bounds the eigenvalue shift.

