The theoretical framework presented herein reconceives social networks as living mathematical structures—Riemannian manifolds evolving under discrete Ricci flow, transport networks redistributing knowledge via optimal transport, hypergraphs capturing team synergies, mean-field games coordinating millions of autonomous agents, spectral filters processing global signals, and stochastic block models uncovering latent communities. Every major claim is fortified by a complete proof architecture, from Cheeger inequalities and Sinkhorn convergence to nonbacktracking spectral thresholds and master equation solvability. The stability of the unified multi-scale system ensures that the interplay of curvature, entropy, and control does not destabilize the global knowledge ecosystem.

The role of the algorithm is no longer to rank content by engagement, but to reduce the intrinsic distance—measured in Wasserstein metrics, spectral gaps, and entropy-weighted divergences—between problems and their solutions. This mathematical scaffold is ready for implementation, offering a rigorous blueprint for the cognitive infrastructure of a multiplanetary civilization.

