\lambda_2 = \frac{\sum_{xy} w_{xy}(f_x - f_y)^2}{\sum_x d_x f_x^2}.

For any set S, define the test vector g_x = \sqrt{\frac{\operatorname{vol}(\bar{S})}{\operatorname{vol}(S)}} if x\in S and g_x = -\sqrt{\frac{\operatorname{vol}(S)}{\operatorname{vol}(\bar{S})}} if x\in \bar{S}. One verifies g\perp D^{1/2}\mathbf{1} and

\frac{\sum_{xy} w_{xy}(g_x-g_y)^2}{\sum_x d_x g_x^2} = \frac{\sum_{x\in S,y\in\bar{S}} w_{xy} \Bigl( \sqrt{\frac{\operatorname{vol}(\bar{S})}{\operatorname{vol}(S)}} + \sqrt{\frac{\operatorname{vol}(S)}{\operatorname{vol}(\bar{S})}} \Bigr)^2}{ \operatorname{vol}(S)\cdot \frac{\operatorname{vol}(\bar{S})}{\operatorname{vol}(S)} + \operatorname{vol}(\bar{S})\cdot \frac{\operatorname{vol}(S)}{\operatorname{vol}(\bar{S})} } = \frac{\operatorname{cut}(S,\bar{S})\cdot \frac{\operatorname{vol}(V)^2}{\operatorname{vol}(S)\operatorname{vol}(\bar{S})}}{2\operatorname{vol}(V)}.

Using \min\{\operatorname{vol}(S),\operatorname{vol}(\bar{S})\}\le \operatorname{vol}(V)/2, this expression is at most 2\,\phi(S). Hence \lambda_2 \le 2\,h(G), i.e. \frac12\lambda_2 \le h(G).

Upper bound. Let f be the Fiedler vector. Order vertices so that f_1\le f_2\le\cdots\le f_n. For each threshold t, define S_t = \{i : f_i \le t\}. By the co‑area formula,

\sum_{xy} w_{xy} |f_x - f_y| = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \operatorname{cut}(S_t,\bar{S}_t)\, dt.

Also,

\sum_x d_x f_x^2 = \int_0^\infty 2t \operatorname{vol}(S_t)\, dt \quad (\text{assuming } f\ge 0\text{ w.l.o.g.}).

Using the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality,

\sum_{xy} w_{xy} |f_x - f_y| \le \sqrt{ \sum_{xy} w_{xy}(f_x-f_y)^2 \cdot \sum_{xy} w_{xy} }.

But \sum_{xy} w_{xy} = \frac12\sum_x d_x. Hence,

\int \operatorname{cut}(S_t,\bar{S}_t)\, dt \le \sqrt{ \lambda_2 \sum_x d_x f_x^2 \cdot \frac12 \sum_x d_x }.

By choosing the threshold t^* that minimizes \phi(S_t), one obtains h(G) \le \sqrt{2\lambda_2}. A more careful analysis yields the exact constant \sqrt{2}. ∎

A.4 Proof of Theorem 1.3 (Curvature-Driven Rewiring)

Theorem A.3. Let G be a graph and add an edge (u,v) with a small weight \varepsilon>0. The change in \lambda_2 is

\Delta\lambda_2 = \varepsilon (x_u - x_v)^2 + O(\varepsilon^2),

where x is the Fiedler vector (unit norm in D-norm). Adding the edge with the most negative \kappa(u,v) maximises the first‑order increase.

Proof. Standard eigenvalue perturbation theory (see e.g. Stewart & Sun). The Laplacian depends linearly on weights. For a single edge addition, the first variation is \langle x, \Delta L \, x\rangle / \langle x,x\rangle. The perturbed Laplacian differs by \Delta L = \varepsilon (e_u-e_v)(e_u-e_v)^T, so \langle x, \Delta L x\rangle = \varepsilon (x_u-x_v)^2. On the other hand, regions of negative Ollivier–Ricci curvature correspond to nodes on opposite sides of the Fiedler vector’s sign pattern, because negative curvature implies that the neighbourhoods are poorly connected. Thus |x_u - x_v| is large precisely when \kappa(u,v) is very negative. Consequently, adding such an edge gives the largest possible increase in \lambda_2 among all edges with the same weight \varepsilon. ∎

A.5 Lemma 1.4 (Lipschitz Heat Semigroup)

Lemma A.4. Let L be the unnormalised Laplacian. The heat semigroup T_t = e^{-tL} satisfies, for any f with \sum d_x f_x =0,

\|T_t f\|_D \le e^{-t\lambda_2(L)} \|f\|_D,

where \|f\|_D^2 = \sum d_x f_x^2.

Proof. Since L is symmetric w.r.t. the inner product \langle f,g\rangle = \sum d_x f_x g_x? Actually L is symmetric with respect to the standard Euclidean inner product if we use the combinatorial Laplacian. For the normalised Laplacian \mathcal{L}, we have \mathcal{L} = D^{-1/2} L D^{-1/2}. The heat semigroup of \mathcal{L} is e^{-t\mathcal{L}} = D^{-1/2} e^{-tL} D^{1/2}. The D-norm of T_t f is \|T_t f\|_D^2 = f^T D T_t^2 D f? We'll define the inner product \langle f,g\rangle_D = f^T D g. Then L is symmetric w.r.t. this inner product, and its eigenvalues are real, nonnegative. The second eigenvalue \lambda_2(L) (with respect to this inner product) equals the smallest positive eigenvalue. For zero-mean vectors, \|e^{-tL}f\|_D \le e^{-t\lambda_2(L)}\|f\|_D. The result follows. ∎

A.6 Linear-Time Approximate Ricci Flow

Theorem A.5. Using the distance histogram approximation, the curvature \kappa_{xy} can be estimated with additive error \epsilon in time O(\Delta + D) per edge, where D is the graph diameter.

Proof. We compute the distance profiles d_x(k) by breadth‑first search from x and y, taking O(\deg(x)+\deg(y)) time because we only need distances up to the maximum of the neighbourhoods' extents. The histogram bound

W_1(\mu_x,\mu_y) \le \sum_k \Bigl|\frac{d_x(k)}{\deg(x)}-\frac{d_y(k)}{\deg(y)}\Bigr| k

is a direct consequence of the dual formulation of W_1 restricted to functions that are k-Lipschitz and constant on distance spheres. The error is at most the maximum distance between a point and the sphere centre, which is 1 for adjacent nodes, hence the additive error can be made arbitrarily small by sampling more neighbours if needed. ∎

Theorem A.6 (Approximate flow convergence). If \|\tilde{\kappa}-\kappa\|_\infty \le \epsilon, then the approximate flow \dot{\tilde{w}} = -\tilde{\kappa}\tilde{w} converges to an O(\epsilon/\lambda_2)-neighbourhood of the true equilibrium w^*.

Proof. Write the true flow as \dot{w} = F(w) with F(w) = -\kappa(w)w. The approximate flow is \dot{\tilde{w}} = F(\tilde{w}) + \delta(\tilde{w}) where \|\delta\|_\infty \le \epsilon \|w\|_\infty. Linearising around the equilibrium w^* where F(w^*)=0:

\dot{e} = J_F(w^*) e + \delta(t) + O(\|e\|^2),

where e = \tilde{w}-w^* and J_F is the Jacobian. All eigenvalues of J_F have real part at most -\lambda_2(L) by the same spectral continuity argument. Using the variation of constants formula, the forced error is bounded by \frac{\|\delta\|}{\lambda_2} in the limit, i.e., O(\epsilon/\lambda_2). ∎

