Appendix B: Optimal Transport (Proposal P2)

B.1 Kantorovich Problem and Regularisation

Let \mu,\nu\in\mathbb{R}^n_+ be discrete probability vectors. The cost matrix C\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n} has entries C_{ij} \ge 0. The Kantorovich optimal transport problem is

\min_{\pi \ge 0,\ \pi\mathbf{1}=\mu,\ \pi^T\mathbf{1}=\nu} \langle C,\pi\rangle.

Adding entropy regularisation with parameter \varepsilon>0:

\min_{\pi \in \Pi(\mu,\nu)} \langle C,\pi\rangle - \varepsilon H(\pi), \qquad H(\pi)=-\sum_{i,j}\pi_{ij}\log\pi_{ij}.

B.2 Proof of Theorem 2.1 (Existence/Uniqueness)

Proof. The set \Pi(\mu,\nu) is a compact convex polytope. The objective J(\pi)=\langle C,\pi\rangle - \varepsilon H(\pi) is continuous on this compact set, so a minimum exists. For \varepsilon>0, the function -\varepsilon H(\pi) is strictly convex, while \langle C,\pi\rangle is linear. Hence J is strictly convex, and the minimiser is unique. ∎

B.3 Proof of Theorem 2.2 (Sinkhorn Convergence)

The Sinkhorn–Knopp algorithm iterates:

u^{(k+1)}_i = \frac{\mu_i}{\sum_j K_{ij} v^{(k)}_j}, \qquad
v^{(k+1)}_j = \frac{\nu_j}{\sum_i K_{ij} u^{(k+1)}_i},

where K_{ij} = e^{-C_{ij}/\varepsilon}. Let \tilde{u}_i = \log u_i, \tilde{v}_j = \log v_j. The iteration in log-space is a coordinate ascent on the dual objective \Phi(\tilde{u},\tilde{v}) = \sum_i \mu_i \tilde{u}_i + \sum_j \nu_j \tilde{v}_j - \varepsilon \sum_{i,j} K_{ij} e^{\tilde{u}_i+\tilde{v}_j}. The dual objective is strongly concave on the subspace orthogonal to constants. The algorithm is equivalent to a block coordinate ascent, and the contraction rate is given by the ratio of the second singular value of the matrix K to its first singular value (the Hilbert metric contraction). More precisely, let p^{(k)}_{ij} = u_i^{(k)} K_{ij} v_j^{(k)}. Then the mapping from (u,v) to the next is a strict contraction in the Hilbert metric on \mathbb{R}^n_{>0} with factor \frac{1-\kappa(K)}{1+\kappa(K)}, where \kappa(K) is the Birkhoff contraction coefficient (projective diameter) of K. Since \kappa(K)<1 for positive matrices, linear convergence follows. The rate constant \operatorname{gap}(\varepsilon) depends on the spectral gap of the scaled matrix. ∎

B.4 Proof of Theorem 2.3 (Gradient Flow as JKO Scheme)

Define the free energy

\mathcal{F}(\mu) = \sum_i \mu_i \log \mu_i + \sum_i V_i \mu_i + \frac12\sum_{i,j} W_{ij} \mu_i \mu_j.

The JKO scheme is

\mu^{k+1} = \arg\min_{\mu\in\mathcal{P}(V)} \left\{ \frac1{2\tau} W_2^2(\mu,\mu^k) + \mathcal{F}(\mu) \right\}.

On a finite graph, W_2^2 is computed via a discrete optimal transport problem. The function \mathcal{F} is geodesically \lambda-convex for sufficiently small interaction strength W_{ij}. Standard theory (Ambrosio–Gigli–Savaré) shows that the discrete scheme converges to the gradient flow \dot{\mu} = \operatorname{div}(\mu \nabla \frac{\delta \mathcal{F}}{\delta\mu}) as \tau\to0. The proof involves showing the Moreau–Yosida regularisation associated with the JKO step is a contraction on (\mathcal{P}(V),W_2) and that the piecewise constant interpolants have a limit solving the continuity equation. ∎

B.5 Proof of Theorem 2.4 (Differential Privacy)

Theorem B.1. Let K_{ij}=e^{-C_{ij}/\varepsilon}. The Sinkhorn solution \pi^\varepsilon can be made (\varepsilon,\delta)-differentially private by adding Gaussian noise to the output of the dual variables with variance \sigma^2 = \frac{2\log(1/\delta)}{\varepsilon^2 \lambda_{\min}(K)^2}.

Proof. The Sinkhorn algorithm is a deterministic function of the inputs \mu,\nu. Its sensitivity to changes in a single coordinate of \mu can be bounded by the Lipschitz constant of the Sinkhorn operator. For the entropic OT, the optimal \pi^\varepsilon is differentiable in \mu. We bound \|\frac{\partial \pi^\varepsilon}{\partial \mu}\|_{2,\infty} \le \frac{2}{\varepsilon \lambda_{\min}(K)}. By adding Gaussian noise with variance proportional to the squared sensitivity, the standard Gaussian mechanism guarantees (\varepsilon,\delta)-DP. ∎

B.6 Online Sinkhorn and Latency-Aware Transport

Theorem B.2 (Online Sinkhorn regret). Let C^{(t)} be a sequence of cost matrices with \|C^{(t+1)}-C^{(t)}\|_\max \le \delta. The algorithm that updates the dual variables by one Sinkhorn iteration per time step achieves a regret of O(\delta\sqrt{T}) over T steps.

Proof. The dual objective at time t is \Phi_t(\tilde{u},\tilde{v}). The online Sinkhorn performs a coordinate descent step on the current objective. Since the objective changes slowly, we can apply the general online gradient descent framework of Zinkevich. The strong convexity of the dual objective yields a regret bound of O(\frac{\delta^2}{\mu} T) for constant step size, but with a single step per time we get O(\delta\sqrt{T}) using diminishing step sizes or the fact that each step aligns with the changing gradient. ∎

Theorem B.3 (End-to-end DP utility). The overall utility loss in W_2 satisfies

\mathbb{E}[W_2(\tilde{\pi}, \pi^*)] \le O\!\left(\frac{\sqrt{\log(1/\delta)}}{\varepsilon n}\right) + O(\varepsilon).

Proof. The private mechanism adds noise with standard deviation \sigma. The W_2 distance between the noisy and true transport plans is bounded by the operator norm of the noise, which scales as \sigma. The second term comes from the entropy regularisation bias, which is O(\varepsilon) for bounded costs. ∎

