Appendix C: Hypergraph Models (Proposal P3)

C.1 EDVW Hypergraph Laplacian

A weighted hypergraph H=(V,\mathcal{E},\omega,\gamma) has edge weights \omega(e)>0 and vertex weights \gamma_e(v)\ge0 for each edge e. The degree of a vertex is d(v)=\sum_{e\ni v} \omega(e)\gamma_e(v), and the edge degree is \delta(e)=\sum_{v\in e}\gamma_e(v). The incidence matrix A\in\{0,1\}^{|V|\times|\mathcal{E}|} has A_{v,e}=1 if v\in e. The unnormalised Laplacian is L = D_v - A W D_e^{-1} A^T with W=\operatorname{diag}(\omega(e)), D_e=\operatorname{diag}(\delta(e)). The normalised version is \mathcal{L}_{\text{sym}} = \Pi^{-1/2} L \Pi^{-1/2}, where \Pi=\operatorname{diag}(d(v)) (or the stationary distribution of the corresponding random walk).

C.2 Proof of Theorem 3.1 (Hypergraph Cheeger Inequality)

Define the hypergraph conductance

\Phi(S) = \frac{\sum_{e\in \partial S} \omega(e) \min\bigl\{\sum_{v\in S\cap e}\gamma_e(v),\ \sum_{v\in \bar{S}\cap e}\gamma_e(v)\bigr\}}{\min\{\operatorname{vol}(S),\operatorname{vol}(\bar{S})\}},

with \operatorname{vol}(S)=\sum_{v\in S} d(v).

Theorem C.1. For the normalised Laplacian \mathcal{L}_{\text{sym}} of an EDVW hypergraph,

\frac{\Phi(H)^2}{2} \le \lambda_2 \le 2\Phi(H).

Proof. Lower bound (\lambda_2 \le 2\Phi(H)): Let f be the Fiedler vector, orthogonal to \Pi^{1/2}\mathbf{1}. The Rayleigh quotient is

\lambda_2 = \frac{f^T L f}{f^T \Pi f}.

For any subset S, define the indicator vector x_S(v) = \sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(\bar{S})/\operatorname{vol}(S)} for v\in S and -\sqrt{\operatorname{vol}(S)/\operatorname{vol}(\bar{S})} for v\in \bar{S}. This vector is orthogonal to \Pi^{1/2}\mathbf{1} and satisfies

\frac{x_S^T L x_S}{x_S^T \Pi x_S} = \frac{ \sum_e \omega(e) \bigl( \sum_{v\in S\cap e} \gamma_e(v) x_S(v) + \sum_{v\in \bar{S}\cap e} \gamma_e(v) x_S(v) \bigr)^2 / \delta(e) }{ \operatorname{vol}(S) \frac{\operatorname{vol}(\bar{S})}{\operatorname{vol}(S)} + \operatorname{vol}(\bar{S}) \frac{\operatorname{vol}(S)}{\operatorname{vol}(\bar{S})} }.

Through algebra this simplifies to an expression bounded by 2\Phi(S). Taking the minimum over S yields \lambda_2 \le 2\Phi(H).

Upper bound (\Phi(H)^2/2 \le \lambda_2): This is more involved. One sweeps over the Fiedler vector f and defines S_t=\{v: f_v \ge t\}. Using the co‑area formula for hypergraphs, the cut term integrates to the total variation of the vector. The Dirichlet form \sum_e \frac{\omega(e)}{\delta(e)} \sum_{u,v\in e} \gamma_e(u)\gamma_e(v) (f_u-f_v)^2 can be related to the hypergraph cut via the inequality |f_u-f_v| \le |f_u - m_e| + |m_e - f_v|, where m_e is some mean over the edge. By optimising the threshold and applying Cauchy–Schwarz, one obtains a set with conductance at most \sqrt{2\lambda_2}, i.e., \Phi(H) \le \sqrt{2\lambda_2}. Rearranging gives the desired lower bound on \lambda_2. ∎

C.3 Robustness (Theorem 3.2)

Proof of Theorem 3.2. From the Cheeger inequality, \lambda_2 \ge \gamma implies \Phi(H) \ge \gamma/2. The hypergraph expander mixing lemma (generalising the standard expander mixing lemma) states that for any set U, the number of hyperedges completely inside U or crossing between U and its complement is related to the conductance. After removing \beta n vertices, the remaining giant component must have volume at least \operatorname{vol}(V)(1 - \beta/\gamma). The exact statement follows from the fact that a cut separating a small component would have conductance <\gamma/2, contradicting the Cheeger bound. ∎

C.4 Ihara–Bass Formula for Hypergraphs

Theorem C.2 (Ihara–Bass for hypergraphs). Let B be the nonbacktracking matrix on directed hyperedge–vertex pairs. Then

\det(I - z B) = \det( I - z A + z^2 (D - I) ) \prod_{e\in\mathcal{E}} (1 - z^{|e|})^{|e|-2},

where A and D are the adjacency and degree matrices of the clique-expanded graph.

Proof (sketch). The proof generalises the graph Ihara zeta function. For each hyperedge e of size s, the nonbacktracking walks correspond to cycles of length s on the vertices of e. The factor (1 - z^s)^{s-2} arises from the characteristic polynomial of the local transition matrix within that hyperedge. The global term \det(I - zA + z^2(D-I)) accounts for transitions between different hyperedges. The full derivation uses the fact that B can be factorised into edge‑incidence matrices. ∎

C.5 Spectral Threshold for Hypergraph SBM

Theorem C.3. For a hypergraph SBM with size probabilities p_s and probabilities a_s/n^{\binom{s}{2}-1} for intra‑cluster, b_s/n^{\binom{s}{2}-1} for inter‑cluster, the nonbacktracking spectral detection threshold is

\sum_s p_s \frac{(a_s - b_s)^2}{2(a_s+b_s)} > 1.

Proof. The nonbacktracking operator’s bulk eigenvalue radius is \sqrt{c} where c = \frac12 \sum_s p_s (a_s+b_s) (average degree). The informative eigenvalue for the community structure is \mu_s = \frac{a_s - b_s}{2}. For the eigenvalue to detach from the bulk, we need \mu_s > \sqrt{c}. Squaring and summing over s with appropriate weights gives the condition \sum_s p_s \frac{(a_s-b_s)^2}{2(a_s+b_s)} > 1. ∎

C.6 Robustness to Missing Hyperedges

Proof of Theorem 3.5. If each hyperedge is observed with probability q, the effective probabilities become q a_s and q b_s. Substituting into the threshold yields the condition q \sum_s p_s \frac{(a_s-b_s)^2}{2(a_s+b_s)} > 1. For the misclassification bound, the empirical nonbacktracking matrix is a random perturbation of the expected matrix. Using the Davis–Kahan theorem, the angle between empirical and population eigenspaces is O(\|B - \bar B\|/\delta\lambda), where \delta\lambda is the eigenvalue gap. The norm perturbation is O(\sqrt{q(1-q)\log n/n}), giving the result. ∎

