Appendix D: Spectral Controllability (Proposal P4)

D.1 Linear System and Gramian

Consider \dot{x} = -L x + B u. The controllability Gramian is

W_c(T) = \int_0^T e^{-L t} B B^T e^{-L^T t} dt.

The system is controllable if \operatorname{rank}(W_c(T))=n. The energy to reach x_f is x_f^T W_c^{-1} x_f.

D.2 Proof of Theorems 4.1–4.4

Theorem D.1 (Fiedler control). The convergence time to consensus scales as 1/\lambda_2(L+BKB^T).

Proof. The solution is x(t) = e^{-(L+BKB^T)t} x(0). The slowest mode decays as e^{-\lambda_2 t}. ∎

Theorem D.2 (Minimum energy selection). Choosing control nodes with largest Fiedler vector entries minimizes the energy.

Proof. Expand the Gramian in the eigenbasis: W_c = \sum_{k=2}^n \frac{1-e^{-2\lambda_k T}}{2\lambda_k} v_k v_k^T. The inverse is W_c^{-1} = \sum \frac{2\lambda_k}{1-e^{-2\lambda_k T}} v_k v_k^T. The energy for a given target becomes a sum weighted by  (v_k^T B B^T v_k). To minimise the trace of W_c^{-1}, we want v_k^T B B^T v_k largest for the smallest \lambda_k, i.e., the Fiedler vector. ∎

Theorem D.3 (Controllability/Observability duality). (L,B) is controllable iff (L^T, B^T) is observable, and the Gramian satisfies the Lyapunov equation -L W_c - W_c L^T + B B^T = 0 (as T\to\infty).

Proof. Standard result; follows from linear systems theory.

Theorem D.4 (Dynamic weights as control). Parametrising edge weights w_{ij}=\phi(\theta_{ij}) transforms the system into a nonlinear one. Linearisation around consensus yields a linear model in the parameters \theta, and optimal parameters are given by a least-squares problem. ∎

