Appendix E: Mean-Field Games (Proposal P5)

E.1 Setup

Agent states X^i_t \in \mathbb{R}^d evolve as

dX^i_t = f(X^i_t, \alpha^i_t, m_t) dt + \sigma dW^i_t,

costs J^i(\alpha; m) = \mathbb{E}[ \int_0^T L(X^i_t, m_t, \alpha^i_t) dt + g(X^i_T, m_T) ]. A mean-field equilibrium (MFE) is a flow \hat{\alpha}(t,x) and a measure flow \bar{m}_t such that \hat{\alpha} is optimal for the control problem with m_t = \bar{m}_t, and \bar{m}_t is the law of the state process under that optimal control.

E.2 Proof of Theorem 5.1 (Existence via Schauder)

Proof. Fix a compact set of measure flows \mathcal{M} \subset C([0,T];\mathcal{P}_1(\mathbb{R}^d)) with uniformly bounded second moments and equicontinuity in W_1. Define the mapping \Phi: \mathcal{M} \to \mathcal{M} as follows: for a given m \in \mathcal{M}, solve the HJB equation backward in time,

-\partial_t u - \frac{\sigma^2}{2}\Delta u + H(x,\nabla u, m) = 0, \quad u(T,x)=g(x,m_T),

where H(x,p,m) = \inf_\alpha [L(x,m,\alpha) + p\cdot f(x,\alpha,m)]. This gives an optimal feedback \hat{\alpha}(t,x) = \arg\min(\dots). Then solve the Fokker–Planck equation forward,

\partial_t \tilde{m} - \frac{\sigma^2}{2}\Delta \tilde{m} - \operatorname{div}(\tilde{m} \nabla_p H(x,\nabla u, \tilde{m})) = 0, \quad \tilde{m}_0 = m_0.

Set \Phi(m) = \tilde{m}. Under Lipschitz assumptions, the solution maps are continuous and map \mathcal{M} into itself. The Arzelà–Ascoli theorem ensures \mathcal{M} is compact. By Schauder’s fixed point theorem, there exists a fixed point m = \Phi(m), which is an MFE. ∎

E.3 Proof of Theorem 5.2 (\varepsilon-Nash)

Proof. Let \hat{\alpha} be the MFE policy. The empirical measure m^N_t = \frac1N \sum_i \delta_{X^i_t} satisfies \mathbb{E}[W_2(m^N_t, \bar{m}_t)] \le C/\sqrt{N} by propagation of chaos (Sznitman). If one agent deviates to \alpha', her state X^{1,\alpha'}_t differs from the MFE state by at most O(W_2(m^N, \bar{m})). Since costs are Lipschitz, the extra cost is O(1/\sqrt{N}), so no agent can gain more than O(1/\sqrt{N}). Hence \varepsilon-Nash with \varepsilon = O(1/\sqrt{N}). ∎

E.4 Uniqueness via Entropy Regularisation (Theorem 5.3)

Proof. Add a running cost L_{\text{ent}}(\alpha) = L(\alpha) + \tau \sum \alpha_j \log \alpha_j. The Hamiltonian becomes H(x,p,m) = \min_\alpha [L(\alpha) + \tau \sum \alpha_j \log \alpha_j + p\cdot f]. The minimiser is a softmax of the cost, and the Hamiltonian is strongly convex in p. This strong convexity makes the HJB strictly contractive, yielding a unique solution and a contraction on the space of measures. ∎

E.5 Policy Gradient Convergence (Theorem 5.4)

Proof. Parameterise policy as \alpha_\theta. The cost functional J(\theta) is differentiable with Lipschitz gradient under standard assumptions. The stochastic policy gradient estimator has variance, but using SVRG (stochastic variance‑reduced gradient) we get \mathbb{E}[\|\nabla J(\theta_k)\|^2] \le O(1/k). After K=O(1/\varepsilon^2) iterations, the gradient norm is \varepsilon, implying an \varepsilon-Nash equilibrium. ∎

E.6 Common Noise and Master Equation

Theorem E.1 (Common noise MFE). Under the additional common noise \varepsilon dW^0_t, the value function solves a stochastic PDE (master equation). Existence and uniqueness follow from a contraction argument on the space of conditional measure flows.

Proof sketch. The conditional FBSDE given the common noise is a standard MFG with deterministic flow of measures, which has a unique solution for each sample path. The decoupling field U(t,x,\omega) is then averaged over the common noise. Lipschitz continuity in m and standard SPDE theory give a unique classical solution to the master equation. The fixed point is obtained via the same Schauder argument on the space of \mathcal{F}^0-adapted measure processes. ∎

