Appendix F: Stochastic Block Models (Proposal P6)

F.1 SBM and Nonbacktracking Matrix

For a graph with adjacency matrix A, the nonbacktracking matrix B is a 2m \times 2m matrix indexed by directed edges i\to j, with entries B_{(i\to j),(k\to l)} = 1 if j=k and i\neq l, 0 otherwise.

F.2 Proof of Theorem 6.1 (Exact Recovery Threshold)

Proof. For the symmetric SBM with two equal-sized communities and parameters a,b, the nonbacktracking matrix has one leading eigenvalue \mu_2 = (a-b)/2 associated with the community structure, and the bulk is confined to the circle of radius \sqrt{\frac{a+b}{2}}. For exact recovery, the informative eigenvalue must be isolated, and its eigenvector must correlate with the community labels. The condition for the eigenvalue to escape the bulk is \mu_2 > \sqrt{\frac{a+b}{2}}, i.e., \sqrt{a} - \sqrt{b} > \sqrt{2}. Necessity follows from an information‑theoretic lower bound. ∎

F.3 SDP Exact Recovery (Theorem 6.2)

Proof. The SDP \max_{Z\succeq 0, Z_{ii}=1} \langle A, Z \rangle is a relaxation of the Max-Cut formulation. A dual certificate is constructed by setting Y = \mathbb{E}[A] + \lambda (J - I) - \nu \sigma \sigma^T, where \sigma is the true community assignment vector. One shows that for parameters satisfying the threshold, the dual variable satisfies Y - A + \Lambda \succeq 0 with high probability. ∎

F.4 Adversarial Robustness (Theorem 6.3)

Proof. If an adversary modifies O(\log n) edges, the adjacency matrix changes by a sparse matrix with norm O(\sqrt{\log n}). The nonbacktracking spectrum is perturbed by at most the same order, while the gap to the bulk is \Omega(\sqrt{n}). Hence the informative eigenvalue remains isolated, and the eigenvector perturbation is O(\sqrt{\log n}/n), not affecting exact recovery. ∎

