Appendix G: Graph Signal Processing (Proposal P7)

G.1 GFT and Filters

The graph Fourier basis is the eigenvectors u_k of \mathcal{L}_{\text{sym}} with eigenvalues \lambda_k. The GFT of a signal x is \hat{x}_k = \langle x, u_k\rangle. A polynomial filter H(S) = \sum_{k=0}^K h_k S^k with S = I - \mathcal{L}_{\text{sym}} applies spectral modulation.

G.2 Uncertainty Principle (Theorem 7.1)

Proof. The two orthonormal bases are the standard basis \{e_i\} and the Fourier basis \{u_k\}. The mutual coherence is \mu = \max_{i,k} |\langle e_i, u_k\rangle|. For any vector x,

\|x\|_0 \|\hat{x}\|_0 \ge \frac{1}{\mu^2}

is a classical result (Donoho–Stark for frames). ∎

G.3 Filter Stability (Theorem 7.2)

Proof. Write the filter as H(L) = \sum_{k=0}^K h_k L^k. For a perturbation \Delta L, we have (L+\Delta L)^k = L^k + \sum_{j=1}^k \binom{k}{j} L^{k-j} (\Delta L)^j + \cdots. The first-order term is bounded by k \|L\|^{k-1} \|\Delta L\|. Summing with weights yields \sum_k |h_k| k \|L\|^{k-1} \|\Delta L\|. The constant C_H = \sum_{k=1}^K k |h_k| (1+\lambda_{\max})^{k-1} then bounds the error. ∎

G.4 Sampling Theorem (Theorem 7.3)

Proof. Let U_r be the n\times r matrix of the first r eigenvectors. A sampling set S is a subset of indices. The sampled matrix is P_S U_r. If this matrix has full column rank, the signal x = U_r \hat{x} can be recovered from P_S x via \hat{x} = (U_r^T P_S^T P_S U_r)^{-1} U_r^T P_S^T y. The frame condition A I \preceq U_r^T P_S^T P_S U_r \preceq B I ensures stable inversion with condition number \kappa = B/A. ∎

G.5 Influence Maximization with Latency

Theorem G.1 (Submodularity). The latency‑aware influence spread \sigma_L(S) is monotone and submodular.

Proof. For each edge, independently sample a delay \tau_{uv} and a live status (active with delay). For a fixed realisation of these random variables, the set of nodes reachable from S within time T_{\max} is a deterministic function R(S). It is straightforward that R(S) is monotone and submodular (since adding a seed only adds reachable nodes, with diminishing returns). The expected spread \sigma_L(S) = \mathbb{E}[|R(S)|] is then a non‑negative linear combination of submodular functions, hence submodular. ∎

Theorem G.2 (RR-set complexity). To approximate \sigma_L(S) within (1-1/e-\epsilon), O(\frac{n \log n}{\epsilon^2 \operatorname{OPT}}) RR‑sets suffice, where \operatorname{OPT}=\sigma_L(S_{\text{opt}}).

Proof. Generate a random RR‑set from a randomly chosen target node v by simulating the reverse cascade until time T_{\max} or until no further edges are active. The process is a reverse Dijkstra that respects the delay constraints. The expected size of an RR‑set equals the number of nodes that can reach v within time T_{\max}. The Chernoff bound for coverage functions yields the sample complexity. ∎

