Appendix H: Entropy‑Guided Feature Recalibration (Proposal P8)

H.1 Entropy‑Gated Attention and InfoNCE

Let z_v be node embeddings from a GNN, and p(c|v) = \operatorname{softmax}(W z_v)_c. The entropy is H(v) = -\sum_c p(c|v)\log p(c|v). The attention coefficients are

\alpha_{ij} = \frac{ \exp( \langle Q z_i, K z_j \rangle / \sqrt{d_k} ) \cdot (1 - H(v_j)/H_{\max}) }{ \sum_{k\in N(i)} \exp( \langle Q z_i, K z_k \rangle / \sqrt{d_k} ) \cdot (1 - H(v_k)/H_{\max}) }.

The InfoNCE loss for a positive pair (i,j) and negatives \mathcal{N} is

\mathcal{L} = -\log \frac{ \exp( \operatorname{sim}(z_i,z_j)/\tau ) }{ \sum_{k\in\mathcal{N}} \exp( \operatorname{sim}(z_i,z_k)/\tau ) }.

H.2 Variance Reduction (Theorem 8.1)

Proof. Consider the neighbourhood \mathcal{N}(i) and the predicted probability for a given class c. The weighted average using standard attention is \bar{p}_{\text{std}} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{N}(i)|}\sum_j p_j. With entropy gating, the weights are w_j \propto (1 - H(v_j)/H_{\max}). The effective sample size is n_{\text{eff}} = \frac{1}{\sum w_j^2}. Since w_j are smaller for high‑entropy nodes, n_{\text{eff}} is larger than the uniform case, reducing variance. A Taylor expansion shows the variance scales inversely with n_{\text{eff}}, giving the factor (1 - \bar{H}/H_{\max})^2. ∎

H.3 InfoNCE Lower Bound (Theorem 8.2)

Proof. By Jensen,

\log \sum_{k\in\mathcal{N}} e^{\operatorname{sim}(z_i,z_k)/\tau} \le \log |\mathcal{N}| + \frac{1}{\tau} \max_{k} \operatorname{sim}(z_i,z_k).

Thus \mathcal{L} \ge \log|\mathcal{N}| - \frac{1}{\tau} \mathbb{E}_{(i,j)}[\operatorname{sim}(z_i,z_j)]. For a hypergraph, the probability that i,j share a hyperedge depends on the random walk mixing time, which is related to the spectral gap of the hypergraph Laplacian. ∎

H.4 Lipschitz Continuity (Theorem 8.3)

Proof. The GNN layers are of the form Z^{(l+1)} = \sigma( \tilde{D}^{-1/2} \tilde{A} \tilde{D}^{-1/2} Z^{(l)} W^{(l)} ). If \sigma is 1‑Lipschitz and \|W^{(l)}\|\le 1, then the layer is a contraction in Frobenius norm. The perturbation in \tilde{A} induces a perturbation in the normalised adjacency bounded by 2\delta/\lambda_2. Composing L layers yields total Lipschitz constant L_f. ∎

H.5 Gradient Stability (Theorem 8.4)

Proof. The derivative of the entropy gate w.r.t. the logits a_{ij} involves the derivative of softmax and the derivative of H(v_j). The entropy gradient \nabla_z H = W^T (p \circ (\log p + 1)) has norm at most \|W\| \log C. With the scaling 1/\sqrt{d_k}, the gradient magnitude remains O(1/\sqrt{d_k}), avoiding explosion or vanishing. ∎


Appendix I: Integration – Multi‑Scale Coupled System (Proposal P9)

I.1 System Definition

The integrated system is the coupled evolution:

1. Micro (agents): dX_t^i = f(X_t^i, \alpha_t^i, m_t, G_t) dt + \sigma dW_t^i.
2. Meso (network): G_{t+1} = \operatorname{Rewire}(G_t, \kappa_t, \lambda_2(t)) where rewiring aims to minimise the objective \alpha \Phi_{\text{Ric}} + \beta \operatorname{Cut} + \gamma \operatorname{Diversity} and preserve \lambda_2 \ge \gamma.
3. Macro (mean field): m_t = \mathcal{L}(X_t^i \mid G_t).
4. Control: consensus dynamics \dot{x} = -L(G_t)x + Bu.
5. Learning: \theta_{t+1} = \theta_t - \eta \nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_{\text{InfoNCE}}(Z_t; G_t).

I.2 Proof of Theorem 9.1 (Coupled Stability)

Theorem I.1. Assume:

· The micro-MFG is well‑posed and contracts in W_2 with rate \lambda_{\text{mfe}} > 0.
· The graph rewiring operator satisfies \|G_{t+1} - G^*\|_F \le \rho \|G_t - G^*\|_F for some \rho < 1 when \lambda_2 \ge \gamma.
· The GNN learning operator is L_\theta-Lipschitz and satisfies L_\theta < \lambda_{\text{mfe}}.

Then the whole system converges to a neighbourhood of (m^*, G^*, \theta^*).

Proof. Define a Lyapunov function

\mathcal{V}(t) = W_2(m_t, m^*)^2 + \|G_t - G^*\|_F^2 + \|\theta_t - \theta^*\|^2.

The micro dynamics give W_2(m_{t+1}, m^*) \le e^{-\lambda_{\text{mfe}} \Delta t} W_2(m_t, m^*) + \text{coupling terms}. The network update gives \|G_{t+1} - G^*\|_F \le \rho \|G_t - G^*\|_F + C_{G} \|\theta_t - \theta^*\|. The learning update satisfies \|\theta_{t+1} - \theta^*\| \le (1 - \eta \lambda_L) \|\theta_t - \theta^*\| + \eta L_\theta \|G_t - G^*\| + \eta L_m W_2(m_t, m^*). Combining these with small‑gain arguments, the overall system is input‑to‑state stable if the loop gain is less than 1, which is guaranteed by L_\theta < \lambda_{\text{mfe}}. ∎

I.3 Proof of Theorem 9.2 (End-to-End Privacy-Utility)

Proof. Each component (transport, community detection, GNN) may be made differentially private with parameters (\varepsilon_p,\delta_p). Advanced composition (Dwork–Roth) yields total privacy \varepsilon_{\text{total}} = \sum_p \varepsilon_p, \delta_{\text{total}} = \sum_p \delta_p. The utility loss is measured by the reduction in the effective spectral gap \lambda_2 of the integrated network Laplacian. Each private mechanism adds noise that perturbs the graph structure or the features, and the total perturbation \Delta L_{\text{total}} = \sum_p \Delta L_p satisfies \|\Delta L_{\text{total}}\| \le \sum_p \sigma_p, where \sigma_p is the noise scale. By Weyl's inequality, |\lambda_k(L + \Delta L) - \lambda_k(L)| \le \|\Delta L\|. For the Gaussian mechanism, \sigma_p scales as O(\frac{\sqrt{\log(1/\delta_p)}}{\varepsilon_p n}), giving the stated bound. ∎

